WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.310
It's great to welcome back to the program
today. Shane Dixon Cavenaugh, who's staff

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.260
porter with the Oregonian in Portland. You
might remember that he recently joined us

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:15.590
to talk about a story that, uh, he's been
following for some time now involving a Saudi

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:22.109
nationals, uh, who in the United States are,
um, accused of crimes, sometimes arrested

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:27.930
and they disappear and he'll tell us what
we mean by disappear specifically, uh, before

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.920
they actually face justice. So Shane, first
of all, great to have you back on. Catch us

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.670
up on what we discussed last time to get everybody
up to speed, if you will. Yes,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:42.149
David, thanks so much for having me back on
your show. So as you mentioned, for the last

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:48.670
few months, I've been working on an investigative
series here at the Oregonian looking into

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:54.709
cases where Saudi nationals, most of them
attending university here in the United States,

00:00:54.709 --> 00:01:01.530
um, have been charged with serious crimes
ranging from manslaughter to rape, to possession

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:07.060
of child pornography. And these individuals,
a large number, a growing number of them,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:14.200
uh, have managed to disappear a while facing
prosecution. Essentially these individuals

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:20.340
had been charged with crimes. They end up
posting bail, uh, have been, uh, retained

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.799
private attorneys. And then as they're moving
close to trial, uh, a number of these individuals

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:31.040
